WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.794
Welcome to Module One, Lesson Two: the agent loop and its control policies.

2
00:00:05.124 --> 00:00:07.954
Every agent, in every framework, runs the same loop.

3
00:00:08.754 --> 00:00:12.715
Picture TripWise, a travel startup whose agent books trips overnight.

4
00:00:13.045 --> 00:00:16.387
One morning, the team wakes up to an eighteen-hundred-dollar bill.

5
00:00:17.187 --> 00:00:20.422
A flight-search tool had returned the same error since midnight.

6
00:00:20.752 --> 00:00:23.753
The agent kept calling it, and nothing told it to stop.

7
00:00:24.553 --> 00:00:27.447
Start with the loop: perceive, reason, act, observe.

8
00:00:27.777 --> 00:00:31.865
Gather the state, let the model pick a step, run the tool, and append the result.

9
00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:36.413
Notice that the model only proposes a tool name and arguments.

10
00:00:36.743 --> 00:00:41.557
Your runtime checks the call and runs it, so every security control lives in code.

11
00:00:42.357 --> 00:00:44.118
When a tool fails, don't crash:

12
00:00:44.168 --> 00:00:47.683
return the error as an observation, so the model can re-plan.

13
00:00:48.013 --> 00:00:52.464
But bound the retries, because the same failing call three times means escalate.

14
00:00:53.264 --> 00:00:56.030
A complete design names five control policies.

15
00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:00.939
Termination, tool exposure, state management, error handling, and escalation.

16
00:01:01.739 --> 00:01:03.803
Termination says when the loop stops,

17
00:01:03.853 --> 00:01:06.660
and tool exposure says which tools the model sees.

18
00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:09.457
State management decides what carries forward.

19
00:01:10.257 --> 00:01:14.026
Error handling chooses to retry, fall back, or degrade gracefully.

20
00:01:14.356 --> 00:01:16.463
Escalation hands over to a human,

21
00:01:16.513 --> 00:01:20.386
say after repeated failure or before an irreversible action.

22
00:01:21.186 --> 00:01:25.083
TripWise had no termination policy, and budgets are not optional.

23
00:01:25.413 --> 00:01:27.754
Every loop needs a step cap, a cost cap,

24
00:01:27.804 --> 00:01:30.504
and a timeout, checked before each model call.

25
00:01:31.304 --> 00:01:34.454
Hitting a cap must fire an alert, never fail silently.

26
00:01:34.784 --> 00:01:37.666
Add loop detection for repeated identical calls,

27
00:01:37.716 --> 00:01:40.537
and require each iteration to change the state.

28
00:01:41.337 --> 00:01:45.660
Then a second problem: on long trips, the agent forgot its earlier bookings.

29
00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:50.569
A raw message list collapses by step fifty, as the context window fills.

30
00:01:51.369 --> 00:01:53.820
The fix is structured state: goal, plan,

31
00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:56.695
findings, and completed steps as typed fields.

32
00:01:57.025 --> 00:02:00.901
Summarise older turns, and store big tool outputs by reference.

33
00:02:01.701 --> 00:02:02.424
On the exam,

34
00:02:02.474 --> 00:02:05.453
an agent that works on short tasks but degrades

35
00:02:05.503 --> 00:02:07.901
on long ones needs context management.

36
00:02:08.231 --> 00:02:12.405
It's not a bigger model or more retries, so read on for the full lesson.
